> Crawl does this [ track items the player has left behind ]
> (there, it's called "stash tracking"). The purpose of
> the feature isn't to give you points for items you've left behind, and
> I'm pretty sure it doesn't. What it does do, though, is allow you to
> search all of the items you have ever seen, in-game, and then travel
> automatically to these stashes. In some cases you have to manually
> define something as a stash, but by default, Crawl just keeps track of
> everything and lets you see where it is. Now, this takes some of the
> burden off of the player (which may not be "the NetHack way"), but you
> still have to make the important choices: What to carry, where to leave
> what you don't carry, when to go back to stashes, &c.

     I think that having nethack attempt to do that would be absurd.
Over time, umpteen thousand items can be created and left behind.
Designating particular items could be implemented in a relatively
straightforward manner (although I don't think it's a good idea).

     NetHack already maintains a pair of lists to handle monsters
who are moving between levels (variety of reasons) and objects
which are moving between levels (usually stuff dropped down holes)
and every time you go to another level, it checks those lists to
see whether anything needs to arrive.  Once the Wizard of Yendor
is in play, if he is left alive when you leave whichever level
he's on, he gets moved onto the migrating monsters list instead
of remaining with the deactivated level.  That way he can be
brought into play on your new level when he's needed.  And if
you return to his level before that happens, he gets put back
there as if nothing out of the ordinary had happened.  It would
be feasible to do something similar for special objects.  But
doing so just for end-of-game disclosure seems fairly pointless
(no pun intended...) to me.



